                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_calibrated:    `<strong>That's the read.</strong> The OSL drifted into laissez-faire, so a calibrated posture — direct the weak sections, protect autonomy elsewhere — is exactly the craft this calls for. Now you have to earn the right to tighten anything.`,
                    coach_r1_calibrated_butall: `<strong>Right posture, wrong target.</strong> You named a calibrated mix but then chose to drill every section equally. Directing where there's no need is just signalling you're in charge — the over-leading penalty is now armed.`,
                    coach_r1_directive:     `<strong>These are 82 professionals, not students.</strong> Firm directive control across the whole orchestra may tick quality up, but your trust index just dropped and the over-leading penalty is active. Which interventions actually buy quality — and which just signal that you're in charge?`,
                    coach_r1_enabling:      `<strong>Enabling isn't abdicating.</strong> You've protected everyone's autonomy and avoided the conflict. The orchestra likes you — but the rating is still 62, the authority needs 75 by March, and right now no one is steering. Where, specifically, will you direct?`,
                    coach_r1_unsure:        `<strong>No diagnosis, no plan.</strong> You can't calibrate direction against enablement if you haven't read where the ensemble sits. Decide the posture before you spend a single rehearsal hour.`,
                    coach_r1_norespect:     `<strong>You're spending authority you haven't earned.</strong> Experts grant authority on perceived competence and respect, not hierarchy. Tightening discipline before you've built standing is how the concertmaster conflict ignites.`,
                    coach_r1_alllistening:  `<strong>You've listened — now lead.</strong> Spending the whole round building rapport and never naming a standard leaves the quality problem untouched. Enablement still needs a direction.`,
                    coach_r2_craft:         `<strong>This is the craft.</strong> Clear standards applied where quality demands them, feedback given with respect — discipline and trust rise together. This is leading professionals, not managing them.`,
                    coach_r2_strict:        `<strong>You've added sectionals, mandated bowings, and you're correcting principals in front of the section.</strong> Quality ticks up a little — but the over-leading penalty is now active and trust has collapsed. Controlling skilled experts destroys the discretionary effort that produces excellence.`,
                    coach_r2_loose:         `<strong>You set no standard.</strong> Hands-off feels respectful, but 320 hours with no discipline standard is exactly the laissez-faire that got the rating to 62. Enabling without standards is not leadership.`,
                    coach_r2_overtime:      `<strong>Your plan needs more than 320 hours.</strong> Hour 321 costs €95 a musician and tells the players you can't work within the constraint. Discipline is a budget — if more rehearsal is your only quality lever, you've already lost. What raises the ensemble without another hour of drilling?`,
                    coach_r2_nosectional:   `<strong>You skipped the sectionals.</strong> Full tutti rehearsals can't fix the two drifting sections — that's where targeted sectional time converts to real quality. You spent hours and bought less than you could have.`,
                    coach_r3_coopt:         `<strong>That's co-opting an ally, not surrendering.</strong> A shared interpretive decision and a featured solo gives the concertmaster standing while you hold the discipline standard. The pre-sale is safe and your authority is intact — this is the difference the round teaches.`,
                    coach_r3_holdline:      `<strong>Held with respect.</strong> You acknowledged his standing, kept the standard, and invited him into how it's set. He stays, the section sees a leader who neither caves nor bullies, and trust rises.`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>You gave him the interpretive control he demanded and the gala pre-sale is safe.</strong> But you're a new leader who just publicly backed down in front of the section. What happens the next time you ask for discipline — and what did the other 81 musicians learn about who runs rehearsals?`,
                    coach_r3_rank:          `<strong>“I'm the music director and the decision is final.”</strong> Technically true. He's now disengaged, two principals are updating their CVs, and the soloist's name is off the gala. Authority over professionals isn't granted by title — it's extended by respect. Did you earn any in Round 1?`,
                    coach_r3_bench:         `<strong>A real fallback.</strong> Developing the assistant concertmaster and reprogramming around the ensemble means the gala doesn't live or die on one name — you've cut the contagion risk and shown the section depth.`,
                    coach_r3_poach:         `<strong>A draw, but not your draw.</strong> Rushing in an external star at a high fee plugs the attendance hole but tells your own players their leader replaces rather than develops. It buys a night and costs goodwill.`,
                    coach_r3_noplan:        `<strong>No fallback, and the star walked.</strong> You built the gala on one name and had no plan when he left. Two principals followed and the pre-sale evaporated — contagion you could have contained.`,
                    coach_r4_balanced:      `<strong>Accessible without selling out.</strong> One accessible work framed by the season's serious repertoire lifts occupancy while the critics still see the OSL's identity. You moved both thresholds instead of trading one for the other.`,
                    coach_r4_pops:          `<strong>You stacked the gala with film scores and pops and occupancy is safe.</strong> But the panel that controls the artistic half of your score just watched the OSL abandon its identity — your quality rating fell. You optimised one threshold and broke the other. The authority renews on both.`,
                    coach_r4_purist:        `<strong>Critically uncompromising — and half-empty.</strong> An all-serious gala protects the rating but misses the occupancy number the authority also judges. Credibility you can't fill the hall for doesn't renew the mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_reconcile:     `<strong>That's the defence.</strong> Naming where you directed, where you enabled, and showing quality, attendance and trust moving together is exactly what the panel renews on. You led the craft and you can prove it.`,
                    coach_r4_qualityonly:   `<strong>One number isn't the mandate.</strong> Leading with the rating alone leaves the panel to wonder about the half-empty hall and the bruised ensemble. The authority renews on all three — defend all three.`,
                    coach_r4_excuses:       `<strong>Blaming the inheritance loses the room.</strong> The panel appointed you to fix exactly this season. Asking for more time while pointing at the constraints reads as a leader who didn't lead. Trust — theirs and the orchestra's — just dropped.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
